window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-fffea50be27c9e4a"]={"key":"ny_b1-fffea50be27c9e4a","group":"ny_b1","uid":"fffea50be27c9e4a","rank":3,"title":"cattermole cad records target architecture","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Sgt. Bronwyn Szymanski, preparing the target design for Cattermole Valley Regional Dispatch Authority before Director Hallam Oyekunle closes the vendor discovery phase in six weeks. Gildenhall Logistics and Anvilcross Trading Company are among the member agencies whose existing records must be brought into the shared CAD/records environment. Use the attached stakeholder notes, `cad_records_stakeholder_notes.docx`, and write `target_cad_records_architecture_plan.docx` in the top level of the working directory. Give us one named target configuration and the reasoning that rules out the other buildable configurations. The plan will be used by the frontline employees who have to operate the result, so make the decision, its boundaries, and the operational ownership unambiguous. Two agencies cannot retire their on-premises servers mid-cycle, dispatch cannot absorb extra call-entry latency, CJIS controls restrict where the records tier can sit, and the NIBRS Group A mapping must survive the historical-data cutover for all three agencies. We have only six weeks and a small migration team, so the textbook transition plan is not available; state a defensible way to work within that limit. Also address the edge case where the lowest-latency arrangement conflicts with the records-location controls. Keep the document to six pages or fewer, omit vendor click-by-click instructions and procurement pricing, and do not invent figures that are absent from the notes. End with the decision itself, not a list of future questions.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/cad_records_stakeholder_notes.docx` — Stakeholder interview notes and comparison material describing the three buildable CAD/records configurations, current-server constraints, latency observations, CJIS hosting restrictions, historical-data and NIBRS requirements, migration staffing, and the discovery deadline.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `target_cad_records_architecture_plan.docx` — A decision memorandum and usable target architecture plan for the consolidated CAD/records environment. (Write the file into the top level of the working directory.; Do not exceed six pages.; Name exactly one target configuration.; Base configuration-specific facts and figures on the supplied stakeholder notes.; Do not include vendor click-by-click instructions or procurement pricing.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/cad_records_stakeholder_notes.docx","bytes":40252,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/6c6f753487d142dabccfa4f53c497aa4d22653b61f4964acf17aa60748ff0d8b.docx","sha256":"6c6f753487d142dabccfa4f53c497aa4d22653b61f4964acf17aa60748ff0d8b","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"CAD / Records Consolidation — Stakeholder Notes\nInterview digest  |  Prepared for vendor-discovery boundary setting; compiled 20 August 2026\nDecision context\nCattermole Valley Regional Dispatch Authority will consolidate Gildenhall Logistics, Anvilcross Trading Company, and the third member agency onto one CAD and records platform next fiscal year. Director Hallam Oyekunle needs a target boundary before vendor discovery closes in six weeks. These notes record operational and technical observations; they do not select a configuration.\nShared constraints heard in every interview\nGildenhall Logistics and Anvilcross Trading Company each operate an on-premises records server. Neither server may be retired during the current operating cycle.\nDispatch personnel reported that routing call entry through the proposed central location added noticeable delay. The authority cannot accept extra call-entry latency on dispatch-critical work.\nCJIS-protected records, their replicas and backups, and administrative access must remain within the approved CJIS hosting and access boundary. The notes do not certify any proposed arrangement.\nHistorical records from all three agencies must remain usable after cutover. The NIBRS Group A classification/mapping must remain available and auditable through conversion.\nThe migration team is small. In the six-week discovery window it cannot run a full, repeatedly rehearsed textbook migration program.\nBuildable configurations discussed\nConfiguration\nBuild boundary\nObserved benefit / trade-off\nA — Centralized Core\nCAD operational services and records tier together in the approved central CJIS boundary; retained servers interface to the core during the operating cycle.\nSimple single location for current data and controls. Dispatch call entry routed through the central location showed the extra delay dispatch rejected.\nB — Split-Path Regional Core\nDispatch CAD operational services stay on the low-latency regional dispatch path. The records tier, replicas, backups, and administration remain in the approved CJIS boundary. A controlled interface crosses the boundary.\nPreserves the dispatch path and the records-location boundary, but adds an interface and disciplined coexistence work.\nC — Agency-Local Continuity\nEach agency keeps an operational node and records remain near the agencies while the shared layer coordinates them.\nStrong local continuity and no central call-entry detour. It leaves current records and historical conversion distributed, and placement/control work is harder to govern consistently.\n\nInterview notes\nSgt. Bronwyn Szymanski — Dispatch supervision: Call takers need the CAD operational path to remain direct. A records lookup may be controlled differently, but an active call cannot wait on a round trip caused by routing the call-entry workflow through an unsuitable central location. During interruption, dispatch needs a clear degraded procedure and a way to reconcile what was captured.\nDirector Hallam Oyekunle — Decision authority: Discovery closes in six weeks. I need one target configuration, its boundary, the evidence that vendors must address, and named operational owners. We can reduce the amount of migration activity, but not the controls that protect call handling, records integrity, or CJIS obligations.\nJoaquin Skarsgard — Infrastructure: Both retained on-premises servers have to stay powered and supported through the current operating cycle. They can have a defined transitional role, but we should not create three competing current-record systems. Configuration A is buildable, but its centralized dispatch route is the same route operators objected to. Configuration B isolates that routing decision from records placement.\nWilhelmina Vanterpool — Records manager: Every agency’s history is in scope, not just the two agencies with servers. The conversion must carry a source identifier and preserve the NIBRS Group A mapping or a traceable crosswalk. Uncertain mappings need review; they cannot quietly become a different classification or disappear.\nKenji Zamora — Information security: Treat CJIS alignment as a validation obligation. The records tier is not merely a database: replicas, backups, privileged administration, and support access must be inside the approved boundary and subject to controlled access and logging. A low-latency location outside that boundary cannot become a records replica just because it is convenient.\nAnders Sorensen — Integration lead: A split design is technically buildable if it makes the interface durable, monitored, and reconcilable. When the interface is unavailable, transactions need a controlled queue and later reconciliation; no team should guess which side is current. Retained servers should be restricted from new shared-current updates once cutover occurs.\nSigrid Kaczmarek — Migration coordinator: With the present team, use one representative rehearsal and risk-based sequencing rather than promising repeated full dress rehearsals. Protect active incidents at cutover with a defined handoff or capture-and-replay method, preserve rollback before the irreversible point, and make go/no-go depend on evidence, not the calendar.\nOpen boundary for discovery\nThe future vendor must demonstrate how the chosen boundary will be operated, validated, monitored, and recovered. These notes intentionally do not state a performance target, retention duration, vendor capability, or compliance certification that has not been established.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/target_cad_records_architecture_plan.docx","bytes":42517,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/640730bcf6988c3f27ff6f5c1bf47d7179c63c68b37de79b5367f65b1c477cfe.docx","sha256":"640730bcf6988c3f27ff6f5c1bf47d7179c63c68b37de79b5367f65b1c477cfe","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Target CAD / Records Architecture Plan\nDecision memorandum  |  From: Sgt. Bronwyn Szymanski  |  For: Director Hallam Oyekunle\nDecision at a glance\nRecommendation: build Configuration B — Split-Path Regional Core. Keep dispatch call entry and dispatch-critical CAD transactions on the low-latency regional dispatch path. Put the authoritative current records tier—and every records replica, backup, and privileged administration path—inside the approved CJIS hosting and access boundary. Connect the two paths through a controlled, monitored interface.\nThis is a target boundary for discovery, not a claim of CJIS certification or vendor capability. Before production use, the designated security owner must validate the required controls and authorization evidence. The selected design accepts interface and coexistence discipline as its trade-off because it does not trade away dispatch responsiveness, records placement, or historical integrity.\nScope, system of record, and frontline operating boundary\nArea\nTarget boundary and working rule\nCAD operational path\nCall entry, unit status, incident command, and dispatch-critical transaction handling remain on the direct regional dispatch path. It must not be rerouted through the central records location.\nRecords-data path\nThe consolidated records tier is the single authoritative location for current records after cutover. It resides inside the approved CJIS boundary; access from CAD is only through the controlled interface.\nHistorical data\nHistorical records from Gildenhall Logistics, Anvilcross Trading Company, and the third member agency are converted or made available through the consolidated records design with source lineage and NIBRS Group A mapping/crosswalk evidence.\nRetained servers\nThe Gildenhall and Anvilcross on-premises servers remain temporarily supported during the operating cycle. They are bounded transition components, not competing current systems of record.\n\nFor dispatch staff: enter and manage the active call on the CAD operational path. For records staff: use the consolidated records tier for current record work after cutover. If the interface is delayed or unavailable, follow the controlled queue and reconciliation procedure; do not create parallel updates in a retained server.\nWhy this configuration; why the others are ruled out\nConfiguration\nDecision reasoning\nA — Centralized Core — rejected\nIt creates a clean single central location, but it routes dispatch call entry through the central location—the path dispatch reported as adding unacceptable delay. A simpler control boundary does not justify extra latency on dispatch-critical work.\nB — Split-Path Regional Core — selected\nIt assigns each workload to the governing constraint: direct regional CAD routing for call entry, CJIS-bound records for protected current data. It permits temporary legacy coexistence and a focused migration. Its interface complexity is explicit and controlled through queueing, monitoring, reconciliation, and ownership rules.\nC — Agency-Local Continuity — rejected\nIt supports local continuity, but leaves current records and historical conversion distributed and makes consistent records placement/control governance harder. It also risks extending the retained-server condition into an indefinite multi-authority arrangement.\n\nEdge case resolution: when the lowest-latency location conflicts with records-location controls, latency governs only the CAD operational workload. CJIS records-location controls govern the records-data workload. The low-latency location must not host a records replica, backup, or privileged records administration function. The boundary is crossed by the controlled interface, not by relocating the records tier.\nLogical flow and control boundary\nFlow\nControl and ownership\n1. Dispatch → CAD operational path\nDirect call-entry and active-incident transactions stay regional. Dispatch supervisor owns continuity and degraded-mode invocation.\n2. CAD → records interface\nDurable outbound queue, message identifier, status monitoring, and exception report. Integration lead owns detection and reconciliation.\n3. Interface → CJIS records tier\nRecords updates are processed in the approved boundary; the records tier establishes current-record authority. Records manager owns data validation.\n4. Retained servers → conversion/access\nOne-way extract or controlled historical access only. New shared-current writes are restricted after cutover. Source identifiers remain attached to consolidated representations.\n\nIf real-time synchronization fails, the durable queue preserves the transaction state for controlled replay. The interface monitor raises an exception; the integration lead reconciles message identifiers and timestamps with records staff before the exception is closed. Where a process cannot be synchronized, staff use the approved degraded capture record, then reconcile it into the authoritative records tier. This is a controlled exception path, not permission for duplicate entry.\nSecurity, access, audit, and resilience\nCJIS control boundary: primary records, replicas, backups, recovery copies, and privileged administrative/support access remain inside the approved hosting and access boundary. Security validation is required before production; this plan does not assert certification.\nAccess: role-based, least-privilege roles distinguish dispatch users, records users, and administrative users. Require managed authentication, account provisioning/deprovisioning, and separate approval for privileged access to protected records.\nAudit: log access to protected records and material record changes with accountable identity and time. The security owner defines review and retention evidence before production use.\nAvailability: protect the records tier with backup and restore or a recovery arrangement that remains within the same CJIS boundary. Test restoration evidence before go-live.\nDispatch continuity: a CAD or network interruption invokes the dispatch degraded-mode procedure, using controlled local capture for active work and replay/reconciliation after restoration. Continuity protects availability; access controls protect confidentiality and authorization—both remain required.\nMigration and coexistence: a constrained, defensible path\nThe small team will not promise a full repeated textbook program in six weeks. Instead, it will use a risk-based minimum: one representative rehearsal, a focused conversion of the highest-risk record patterns, and evidence gates that protect dispatch continuity, CJIS controls, and data integrity. Historical scope remains all three agencies even if some lower-risk historical material is staged for controlled later ingestion rather than forcing an unsafe all-at-once cutover.\nStage\nRequired control\nPrepare\nInventory source identifiers and NIBRS Group A mappings/crosswalks; establish extraction read boundaries; assign records reviewers. Label unresolved source conditions as exceptions.\nRehearse once\nRun a representative conversion and reconciliation. Compare source counts/identifiers, inspect mapping results, test interface failure/replay, restore evidence, and dispatch workflow responsiveness.\nCut over active work\nAt the declared handoff, preserve a time-stamped active-incident roster. Capture transactions created during the transition for replay; reconcile every captured item before declaring normal operation. Retain a rollback point before irreversible conversion activation.\nCoexist temporarily\nRestrict retained servers from new shared-current updates. Use them only for their defined transition purpose; use one-way extracts or controlled historical access. Reconcile interfaces and exception queues daily during the transition.\nExit retention\nAfter the operating-cycle restriction ends, Director Oyekunle reviews completion evidence, preservation obligations, and recovery need before authorizing controlled legacy-server disposition. No automatic retirement is assumed.\n\nData quality and lineage: each consolidated historical record must retain the agency/source identifier and a conversion audit reference. Preserve the original NIBRS Group A value or an approved traceable crosswalk. Unmapped, ambiguous, failed, or conflicting records go to a quarantined exception queue; the records manager assigns review and disposition. They are neither silently discarded nor automatically reclassified.\nReadiness evidence, owners, and six-week discovery package\nOwner\nAccountability before production / discovery close\nDirector Hallam Oyekunle\nArchitecture decision authority, go/no-go approval, and escalation decision when a new constraint invalidates the selected boundary.\nSgt. Bronwyn Szymanski, Dispatch supervisor\nDispatch continuity owner; confirms active-incident handoff, degraded-mode procedure, and observed call-entry responsiveness on the chosen operational path.\nWilhelmina Vanterpool, Records manager\nOwns historical completeness reconciliation, source lineage, NIBRS Group A mapping/crosswalk assurance, and exception disposition.\nKenji Zamora, Security owner\nValidates CJIS boundary, access lifecycle, privileged access, logging, and records backup/recovery placement before production use.\nAnders Sorensen, Integration lead\nOwns interface monitoring, durable queue, failure alerting, replay, reconciliation, and retained-server write restrictions.\n\nBy the end of...\nEvidence / decision required\nWeeks 1–2\nFreeze target boundary; confirm retained-server roles; inventory historical sources and NIBRS mappings; define access, audit, and interface evidence.\nWeeks 3–4\nComplete representative rehearsal; demonstrate dispatch-path responsiveness without added central routing; test queue/replay and controlled records access.\nWeeks 5–6\nComplete reconciliation review, recovery evidence, owner sign-offs, and discovery package: target boundary, data scope, operational flows, validation evidence, and unresolved risks with escalation triggers.\n\nGo/no-go is evidence-based, not date-based: dispatch confirms workable active-call and degraded operations; records confirms all three agencies’ reconciliation and NIBRS mapping review; security confirms the required CJIS boundary, access, logging, and recovery evidence; integration confirms queue/replay reconciliation. A newly found condition that would place records outside the boundary, add dispatch call-entry latency, or break data lineage is escalated immediately to Director Oyekunle for change control; it does not get normalized in the field.\nAssumptions and exclusions\nAssumptions (proposed controls, not source facts): the authority will designate the named roles or equivalents, maintain a durable interface queue, and adopt a dispatch degraded-mode procedure. The notes do not establish a numeric latency threshold, a retention period, vendor performance, capacity, or a compliance certification; therefore this plan requires observable validation rather than inventing those facts. Procurement pricing and vendor click-by-click procedures are out of scope.\nDecision\nDirector Oyekunle should approve Configuration B — Split-Path Regional Core as the single target configuration for vendor discovery: direct regional CAD operations for dispatch, a CJIS-bound authoritative records tier, controlled interface-based synchronization, and bounded temporary retention of the Gildenhall and Anvilcross servers. This is the decision.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan names exactly one target configuration as the recommendation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation or decision section","grader_hint":"Confirm that one configuration is explicitly selected and that no second configuration is presented as co-equal."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is stated plainly enough that a frontline reader can tell what architecture will be built.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening summary and decision statement","grader_hint":"A reader should not need to infer the answer from comparative prose."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The selected configuration is supported by a reasoned comparison against each of the other buildable configurations.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Options analysis","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit advantage or disqualifying trade-off for both rejected options, not merely a list of their names."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan traces the recommendation to all four stated design pressures: server-retirement timing, dispatch latency, CJIS records placement, and historical NIBRS preservation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale and constraint traceability","grader_hint":"Each pressure must be connected to a design choice or exclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document ends with a clear decision rather than unresolved alternatives or a list of questions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph or decision box","grader_hint":"The ending may identify accountable actions, but it must close on the selected decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan distinguishes the CAD operational path from the records-data path.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Target architecture description","grader_hint":"Accept clear equivalent terminology such as dispatch transaction path versus records tier."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The architecture keeps dispatch call entry and dispatch-critical transactions on the path best able to avoid added latency.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Architecture rationale","grader_hint":"The plan must connect placement or routing to the latency constraint, not just promise performance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The records tier is placed or handled in a manner consistent with CJIS-controlled hosting and access requirements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security and hosting section","grader_hint":"Require a concrete hosting boundary, trust boundary, or controlled access design; reject vague 'secure cloud' language without controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan treats CJIS alignment as a control obligation and does not casually claim that the proposed arrangement is certified merely because it is selected.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security claims","grader_hint":"Credit bounded language describing required validation, authorization, or controls rather than an unsupported certification assertion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The edge case in which low latency conflicts with records-location controls is explicitly resolved through a split, boundary, routing, or equivalent architecture decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trade-off analysis","grader_hint":"The document must say which requirement governs which workload and how the resulting design avoids simply ignoring one requirement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan preserves the two agencies' on-premises servers during the prohibited mid-cycle retirement period.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition architecture","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit interim role, coexistence arrangement, or retained function for both servers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The retained-server arrangement has a defined boundary so that temporary coexistence is not presented as an indefinite duplicate system of record.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition and governance sections","grader_hint":"Credit a stated purpose, ownership, dependency, or exit condition for the retained systems."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The historical data from all three agencies is included in the migration or access design.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data migration scope","grader_hint":"The plan must cover all three agencies rather than only the two with servers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"NIBRS Group A classification or mapping is preserved through historical-data conversion.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data conversion section","grader_hint":"Require mapping preservation or controlled crosswalk treatment, not just a statement that NIBRS is important."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan requires validation that NIBRS Group A mapping remains intact after conversion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Migration assurance or acceptance criteria","grader_hint":"Accept reconciliation, sample testing, exception review, or equivalent professional validation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan identifies how unmapped, ambiguous, or failed historical records are handled without silently discarding or misclassifying them.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data quality and exception handling","grader_hint":"Look for quarantine, exception queue, review ownership, or an equivalent controlled disposition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The architecture maintains a traceable relationship between source historical records and their consolidated representations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Lineage or audit design","grader_hint":"Credit source identifiers, lineage metadata, audit trail, or a clearly equivalent mechanism."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies a single authoritative location or authority for current records after cutover.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"System-of-record statement","grader_hint":"The wording must establish where current authoritative data resides."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan prevents duplicate or conflicting updates while retained systems and the consolidated platform coexist.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Coexistence controls","grader_hint":"Accept ownership rules, one-way interfaces, write restrictions, reconciliation, or equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed design states what happens to records or transactions that cannot be synchronized in real time.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integration and outage handling","grader_hint":"A usable plan provides a controlled queue, reconciliation, manual fallback, or equivalent rather than leaving the case unspecified."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The migration approach includes a repeatable rehearsal, validation, or rollback concept appropriate to a high-consequence public-safety cutover.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cutover plan","grader_hint":"Credit a dry run, staged conversion, rollback point, or equivalent; do not require a particular tool."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan explicitly addresses cutover continuity for active incidents and records that are being created at the time of transition.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cutover continuity","grader_hint":"Look for active-call handling, freeze window, capture-and-replay, reconciliation, or equivalent treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The cutover design includes a go/no-go decision based on operational readiness rather than calendar date alone.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Readiness or acceptance section","grader_hint":"Require concrete readiness evidence such as test results, data validation, access checks, or dispatch sign-off."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides a defensible fallback for the six-week deadline and small migration team instead of assuming a full textbook program is possible.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resource and schedule rationale","grader_hint":"Credit prioritization, phased scope, focused rehearsal, risk-based sequencing, or equivalent bounded fallback."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The fallback preserves the highest-consequence capabilities and controls rather than reducing effort indiscriminately.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Prioritization rationale","grader_hint":"The plan should visibly protect dispatch continuity, CJIS controls, and data integrity."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan assigns an owner for architecture decision authority or approval.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance section","grader_hint":"Accept a named role or named stakeholder from the brief."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan assigns operational ownership for dispatch continuity during cutover.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles and responsibilities","grader_hint":"Require a role accountable for dispatch operations, not merely 'the team'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan assigns ownership for records-data validation and NIBRS mapping assurance.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles and responsibilities","grader_hint":"Look for a records, data, reporting, or equivalent accountable role."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies who owns security or CJIS control validation before production use.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security governance","grader_hint":"Accept a security officer, CJIS authority, compliance owner, or equivalent role."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes role-based access or least-privilege treatment for dispatch, records, and administrative users.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access-control section","grader_hint":"Credit explicit role separation and least-privilege language or a clear equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes authentication, account lifecycle, or privileged-access controls appropriate to CJIS-protected records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access-control section","grader_hint":"At least one concrete control beyond the word 'secure' is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes audit logging for access or material changes to protected records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security and audit section","grader_hint":"Require identifiable logging scope and a review or retention expectation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The plan distinguishes availability and continuity controls from confidentiality and access controls.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security and resilience sections","grader_hint":"A single generic security paragraph is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The target architecture includes a backup, recovery, or service-continuity approach for the records tier.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resilience section","grader_hint":"Accept backup and restore, replicated service, recovery environment, or equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan addresses how dispatch continues during a CAD or network service interruption.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Business continuity section","grader_hint":"Look for a defined degraded-mode or fallback operating concept."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The proposed resilience arrangement does not undermine the stated CJIS records-location boundary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resilience and security cross-check","grader_hint":"Check backup, replica, and failover locations as well as the primary records tier."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan describes interface or data-flow boundaries between CAD, records, historical data, and retained systems.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Logical architecture","grader_hint":"Accept a concise data-flow narrative or diagram with explanatory labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies how interface failures are detected and reconciled.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integration operations","grader_hint":"Require monitoring, exception reporting, reconciliation, or equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The architecture boundary is understandable to a frontline employee who must operate around it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Assess whether the operational consequences of the design are intelligible without specialist interpretation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan defines what is in scope for the target build and what remains temporarily outside it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope and boundary statement","grader_hint":"Require explicit treatment of retained legacy components and historical data."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies a controlled disposition for legacy servers after their temporary retention period.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition end-state","grader_hint":"Accept retirement criteria, review gate, or a stated later decision point."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The selected design avoids unsupported claims about absent capacity, performance, compliance status, or vendor capability.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Compare factual claims with the supplied notes and identify whether assumptions are labeled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Assumptions and exclusions created to close gaps in the notes are explicitly labeled and do not masquerade as source facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions, scope, or footnotes","grader_hint":"Credit clearly marked assumptions and a distinction between confirmed facts and proposed controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides measurable or observable acceptance conditions for dispatch latency or responsiveness.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Acceptance criteria","grader_hint":"Use a source-provided threshold if available; otherwise credit a testable operational criterion without inventing unsupported numbers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides acceptance conditions for historical-data completeness and NIBRS mapping integrity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Acceptance criteria","grader_hint":"Look for reconciliation, exception thresholds, or sign-off evidence tied to all three agencies."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides acceptance conditions for CJIS access and audit controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Security acceptance criteria","grader_hint":"Credit testable access, logging, review, or authorization evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation makes the trade-offs visible rather than presenting the chosen configuration as costless or universally superior.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision rationale","grader_hint":"Acknowledge meaningful disadvantages and explain why they are acceptable or controlled."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan orders or prioritizes work against the six-week discovery deadline.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Milestones or near-term plan","grader_hint":"Require a sequence or priority tied to the deadline, without requiring a particular calendar format."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies the decisions or evidence that must be available before the vendor discovery phase closes.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Deadline alignment","grader_hint":"Look for target configuration, boundaries, data scope, or validation evidence appropriate to discovery."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes a change-control or escalation path for a newly discovered constraint that would invalidate the selected design.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance or risk section","grader_hint":"Credit an accountable decision gate and escalation trigger."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is written as an operationally usable plan rather than a generic technology essay.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Assess specificity, decision usefulness, and whether a frontline reader can understand what the target arrangement means in practice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document uses consistent terminology for CAD, records, historical data, retained servers, and the target configuration.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Do not award if shifting terms create uncertainty about whether components are distinct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The architecture description is sufficiently concrete to be handed to the vendor for discovery without requiring the author to explain the basic target boundary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Target-state section","grader_hint":"Look for named tiers, placement, interfaces, coexistence boundaries, and migration scope."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted document is no longer than six pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX pagination","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX and count pages; do not count only source paragraphs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not include vendor click-by-click operating instructions.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Confirm that the plan stays at architecture, control, migration, and operational-boundary level."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not introduce procurement pricing or unsupported invented figures.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document compared with source notes","grader_hint":"Allow source-supported figures; check that absent quantities are not fabricated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document has clear headings or visual structure that let dispatch and records personnel locate the decision, target state, transition controls, and responsibilities quickly.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document","grader_hint":"Assess practical scanability, not decorative styling."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is internally coherent: the selected configuration, data flows, security boundary, migration approach, acceptance conditions, owners, and final decision do not contradict one another.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document","grader_hint":"Trace the selected design through every section and deduct only when two statements cannot both be true."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a bounded public-safety technology decision for a regional dispatch authority consolidating three agencies onto one CAD and records platform. The artifact must be useful to dispatch and records personnel while preserving defensible security, migration, and operational controls.","difficulty_sources":["Choosing one defensible architecture from three buildable alternatives","Resolving the latency-versus-CJIS placement edge case","Designing bounded coexistence for two servers that cannot yet be retired","Packaging migration assurance and operational continuity within six weeks and a small team","Preserving historical NIBRS Group A mapping without inventing unsupported facts"],"authoring_plan":{"source_file":{"filename":"cad_records_stakeholder_notes.docx","construction":"Create one realistic stakeholder-notes document containing a short decision context followed by notes from Sgt. Bronwyn Szymanski, Director Hallam Oyekunle, Joaquin Skarsgard, Wilhelmina Vanterpool, Kenji Zamora, Anders Sorensen, and Sigrid Kaczmarek. The notes should establish exactly three buildable configurations with neutral names and enough source-supported facts for comparison, while leaving the professional architecture decision to the solver.","facts_to_include":["Cattermole Valley Regional Dispatch Authority is consolidating three member agencies onto one CAD/records platform next fiscal year.","Vendor discovery closes in six weeks.","Two agencies have on-premises servers that cannot be retired during the current operating cycle.","Dispatch call entry has a documented or observed latency concern if the dispatch path is routed through an unsuitable central location.","CJIS controls constrain where the records tier and its replicas, backups, or administrative access may reside.","Historical data from all three agencies must be retained and made usable after cutover.","NIBRS Group A mapping must remain available and auditable through conversion.","The migration team is small and cannot complete a full, repeated textbook rehearsal program within six weeks.","Each of the three configurations is technically buildable, but each has a distinct trade-off involving latency, server coexistence, CJIS boundary, migration complexity, or operational continuity.","The notes may contain source-supported performance observations, migration counts, or timing estimates, but they must be internally consistent and should not require live internet research."],"design_intent":"Make the source clean and internally consistent, with no hidden corrupt rows or trick data. The difficulty should arise from comparing alternatives, recognizing the collision between latency and records placement, and designing a defensible constrained transition. Do not include a private answer key or wording that dictates the preferred configuration."},"gold_logic":{"target_solution":"A passing reference solution selects one of the three source-described configurations and explains why it best balances the constraints. The exact winning configuration may vary if the rationale is coherent and grounded in the source, but it must preserve dispatch responsiveness, respect CJIS boundaries, retain both legacy servers during the allowed period, and protect historical NIBRS mapping.","required_professional_behaviors":["Separate CAD operational traffic from the records tier when necessary to resolve the latency-versus-CJIS edge case.","Treat the two retained servers as bounded transitional components rather than silently retiring them or creating indefinite competing systems of record.","Define authoritative ownership and synchronization behavior during coexistence.","Use controlled migration validation, lineage, exception handling, and active-incident continuity.","Use a risk-based fallback or phased approach because the migration team and six-week deadline make a full textbook program infeasible.","State accountable owners, readiness evidence, and a go/no-go decision.","Label assumptions and avoid inventing absent numbers or claiming unsupported compliance certification."]},"rubric_derivation":"Items R01-R05 assess the single bounded decision and comparative justification. R06-R20 assess target architecture, security placement, coexistence, and data lineage. R21-R25 assess migration continuity and the explicit time/resource fallback. R26-R36 assess operational ownership, access, audit, and resilience. R37-R50 assess data flows, scope, assumptions, acceptance, governance, and deadline packaging. R51-R58 assess usability, consistency, page and content constraints. All items are credit items; there are no over-production penalties."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"cad_records_stakeholder_notes","filename":"cad_records_stakeholder_notes.docx","purpose":"Stakeholder interview notes and comparison material describing the three buildable CAD/records configurations, current-server constraints, latency observations, CJIS hosting restrictions, historical-data and NIBRS requirements, migration staffing, and the discovery deadline.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this file before drafting the architecture plan; it is the sole supplied source for configuration-specific facts and figures."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"target_cad_records_architecture_plan","filename":"target_cad_records_architecture_plan.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A decision memorandum and usable target architecture plan for the consolidated CAD/records environment.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file into the top level of the working directory.","Do not exceed six pages.","Name exactly one target configuration.","Base configuration-specific facts and figures on the supplied stakeholder notes.","Do not include vendor click-by-click instructions or procurement pricing."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"police_detective_supervisors","sector":"government","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use one domain-oriented reviewer to assess public-safety architecture, CJIS-aware controls, migration reasoning, and operational usability, plus automated inspection for file type, page count, and excluded content. Review the submitted document against the relational criteria and the supplied stakeholder notes.","minimum_reviewers":1,"known_limitations":["The source deliberately permits more than one defensible configuration choice; grading must focus on constraint traceability and internal coherence rather than a single hidden option name.","CJIS compliance should be assessed as bounded control reasoning, not as a legal certification determination."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:38:52+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:38:52+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:38:52+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:38:52+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:38:52+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/fffea50be27c9e4a","declared_deliverables":["target_cad_records_architecture_plan.docx"]};
